 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >I did a little more research on the term Squaw, and it sounds like we may be both right on this issue, Mera.  Since different Native American races have completely different languages, it's quite possible that there are many different words like "squaw" that mean different things in different languages.  In one article I read, it said that in the Northeast (where Native Americans were first encountered, and alot of their language was given to us), Squaw is a term for woman and is not obscene.  However in the southwest, it does have a different, obscene, meaning (btw, a few of the things I read also pointed out that most Native Americans probably wouldn't have given words like that to the White Man).  So while it's quite possible that it could have been used as a derogatory insult, it also could have been just another word, depending on where you went.<br>
<br>
Either way, though, I found a great <a href="http://www.nativeweb.org/pages/legal/squaw.html" target=_blank>article</a> written by a Native American woman who said that rather than trying to eradicate words from use, which doesn't fix the problem, why not simply accept a different meaning?  In the closing of her article she said:<br>
<br>
<blockquote><FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" >quote:</font><hr>Indigenous people must publicly declare that we will no longer allow our words, names, skin color, beliefs, etc., to be used against us. Whenever the word "squaw" is used as an insult, my response is: "I do not accept that definition. Among my people, WOMEN are honored and respected. The sound "squaw," regardless of its spelling,  is OUR word for woman, and it is NOT to be used as an insult! When I hear it spoken by Native peoples, in its proper context, I hear the voices of the ancestors.  I am reminded of powerful grandmothers who nurtured our people and fed the strangers, of proud women chiefs who stood up against them, and of mothers and daughters and sisters who still stand here today. In their honor I demand that our language, our women, and our history, be treated with respect.<hr></blockquote><br>
I don't want to sound like I'm saying that you're wrong.  Insults have as much to do with intent as with the actual words used.  And if you feel that you and your people are being insulted by the use of the word "squaw", then you should have a right to want to see it changed.  I'm just trying to look at all sides of the issue here.<br>
<br>
__________________<br>
If I were creating a world, I wouldn't mess about with butterflies and daffodils! I would've started with lasers, eight o'clock, day one!</font></p>

