 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >I suspect many derogatory words come from non-offensive<br>
beginnings.  I'm sure "Fuck You" is quite friendly in<br>
some circles.  Maybe "squaw" was used non-offensively in<br>
some group at some time, maybe even the original use of<br>
the word.  But I think this is missing the point.<br>
<br>
I think there is enough evidence to show that "squaw" was<br>
used offensively by colonists and Native Americans in<br>
the 1700's and 1800's.  If you think about it, it is just<br>
common sense.  I mean, the colonists were basically hunting<br>
down and killing the Native Americans like animals.  Do you<br>
expect me to believe while they were doing this, they were<br>
refering to the women folk with the most respectful term<br>
they were exposed to?  My, she's a fine squaw.  Her scalp<br>
and those of her fine children will allow me to buy my<br>
*real* woman some nice flowers.  I like the "snap" sound you<br>
get when you step on the squaw's back and pull the last part<br>
of the scalp off.  Yes, I'm sure squaw was used in a respectful way.<br>
<br>
It was obviously used in a very derogatory manner.  And<br>
obviously there are some Native American's who claim<br>
it is offensive in their languages today and during the<br>
genocide.  Not saying it should be removed from some<br>
remote groups who use the word for something nice, but<br>
it obviously should be removed from the names of lakes<br>
and canyons simply because it is very offensive to a<br>
large number of Native Americans.<br>
<br>
To make another point.  The article that Merathena<br>
posted, was under the section "Oddly Enough", as if<br>
changing the names to respect the Native Americans<br>
was *odd*.  What century do we live in again?<br>
<br>
Darksar<br>
-------<br>
some light can never be seen<br>
</font></p>

